I basically agree with you (which is why I brought it up), and it
seemed in the earlier discussion that I was one of the few who
actually got your point.

My only quibble is this: your formulation is most appropriate for
those who start the game with the intent to do "survivor" conduct.
IOW, if you're going to consider yourself as having "lost" if you blow
the conduct, then you're absolutely right: the AoLS is completely
useless, and it may have been helpful to have something else in its
place.

OTOH, if you're simply trying to get an ascension and claim what you
can once it's over, the AoLS may* help you do that, because it gives
you another shot after YASD. You can't claim "survivor" after that
happens, of course, but you can at least claim an ascension, plus
whatever other conducts you happened to keep.

The AoLS discussion is really a subset (probably the most obvious
example) of the "does a conduct really count if you don't decide on it
before you start" discussion. With a total of 5 ascensions under my
belt, I'm hardly at the point where I'd seriously handicap myself from
the get-go. But I do sometimes find myself in a position, say at the
Castle, where I think, "Do I really need to use a wish? Is it really
necessary for me to geno anything?" And then I still might do
it--whatever it is I'm thinking of going without--if I get in a jam,
but if I can get through to the ascension without having done it, then
yes, I'll claim the conduct.

*It's also arguable that once a player has become careful enough to
avoid most forms of instadeath, the AoLS doesn't even really assist in
the ascension itself. Reflection or magical breathing can save you
repeatedly, while the AoLS works only once.
